e table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<1.65).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0336\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0418\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ | $\\,0.0495\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/1b3a9a3891150ae0b187c79e2305d391f719898a9efe42b42e7bf0777d965aa3.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>-a)=P(Z<a)=Φ(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z<1.65).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/fd2f95626ff0a8f1aa59e94e18fddaae63216ee5962f4b7264ea952db76619b6.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z>-1.65).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/94dfdaf777193a2a4b39e7e13c1297c3497c3d4dd474599a16a1bd4f559602b0.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<1.65) & =P(Z>-1.65) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-1.65) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.65) \\\\  & =1-0.0495 \\\\  & =0.9505.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-183811/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a6ee4c7-a51f-5dce-b5c9-2e00acf6bf3e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-183811"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-330650/field-1/c657f549c0528bd64d18c72d2681949c3cca34b61ce37ee512977a8f34a73978" :answer/id #uuid "cacf34bb-97c5-514a-92ac-e5528e3a86ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9484" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330650/field